[03:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bar. That being said, let\'s take a look at it in action. To start Moles in the terminal, you simply type Moles, and you\'ll then see five options: clean,'

[03:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uninstall, optimize, analyze, and status. The first option, clean, is an incredibly comprehensive sweep of the back alleys and hidden corners of macOS'

[03:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'looking for all those redundant, orphaned, non-essential, and leftover files that pile up over time. On my M2 MacBook Pro, a full clean took about 9'

[03:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'minutes and netted me nearly 13 GB of space I could reclaim. Obviously, the amount of space you reclaim will depend on how often you run the app, but you'

[04:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can expect to see substantially more space cleaned if it\'s the first time you\'ve used it. On my clean run through, the user essentials category, which is stuff like app caches, logs, and trash,'

[04:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'freed up about 8 gigs of space. Developer tools was 2.1 gigs of crap, and the browser suite liberated 1 and 1/2 gigs. Unless you\'ve just run out of'

